    "md5": "b6e622b5b80b989c0b90f989f08a1035",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1947,
    "chunk_text": "Hayleys Chairman\u2019s Awards 2025 honours teams driving innovation and business excellence\n\nHayleys PLC Chairman and Chief Executive Mohan Pandithage presents Haycarb PLC with the Overall Winner Award at the Hayleys Chairman\u2019s Awards 2025 for their pioneering work in silicon-carbon anode materials for lithium-ion batteries\n\nHayleys Group, Sri Lanka\u2019s largest and most diversified conglomerate, celebrated groundbreaking achievements of its teams at the Chairman\u2019s Awards 2025, recognising national firsts that advance global priorities in energy transition, food security, resilience and social impact.\n\nThe event, now in its 13th year, reinforced people-led innovation as a driver of resilience and long-term competitiveness across innovation, ESG, quality and lean management and service excellence.\n\nHayleys Chairman and Chief Executive Mohan Pandithage stated, \u201cThese awards are an opportunity to thank our teams for their exceptional commitment and dedication to enriching lives across every touchpoint, from products and solutions that serve Sri Lankan families to world-class exports competing in global markets. These initiatives have strengthened Hayleys\u2019 international standing, deepened our national contribution to the economy and society and showcased the true capability of Sri Lankan enterprise.\u201d\n\nAt the heart of the awards are pioneering projects that establish national firsts while addressing global challenges.\n\nThe Purification Sector saw Haycarb PLC secure the Overall Winner Award for the \u2018Innovation of Specialty Activated Carbon for Silicon-Carbon Composites used as Next Generation Anode Materials in Lithium-Ion Batteries\u2019, through the introduction of the HCE 400 Series to improve energy density and battery life. The initiative also secured the Innovation Category Award, marking a strategic step into advanced materials aligned with the growth trajectory of global battery and energy storage markets."
